  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nPrecipitation Trends...The forecast area remains on tracks to see \nwidely scattered rain showers today as a mid-level shortwave/trough \nand weak surface warm front sweeps across the region. Although deep-\nlayer shear will be around 30-40 kts, a lack in instability (only \naround 100 J/kg) will limit thunderstorm chances to only a few \nrumbles of thunder at times. As the main area of rain gradually \ndeparts the region to the northeast late this evening, another warm \nfront will begin to lift into central and east-central WI. Models \nseem to be in decent agreement this front will produce additional \nisolated/scattered showers and perhaps some elevated storms in these \nareas before these showers/storms and the showers in far northeast \nWI exit by Sunday morning. PWATs will mainly be in the 1.50-1.75 \ninch range, but will increase to 1.75-near 2.00 inches with the \narrival of the secondary warm front. CAMs/short-range models \nindicate the axis of heaviest rain (0.50-1.00 inches) will be across \nthe Northwoods, while mid to long-range models indicate the axis \nwill extend from central into east-central WI. However, ensembles \nare trending towards the short-range model solution. Leaned more \ntowards this solution given the agreement/trends.\n\nThe warm front will gradually lift north on Sunday and become nearly \nstationary over the area on Monday as an upper-level ridge resides \noverhead. This will leave the forecast area in the warm sector with \na surplus of moisture and instability, especially on Monday (MUCAPE \nof 2000-3000 J/kg). Models are in disagreement in precip chances for \nSunday, but the greatest chances should remain focused along the \nwarm front as it lifts north. For Monday, despite the surplus of \nmoisture and instability, mid-level capping may prevent any \nconvection from developing. However, there are some signs of a cold \nfront/shortwave moving over the area sometime later in the day on \nMonday, which would present the opportunity for showers and\nstorms, including strong/severe, to develop. Will need to monitor\nthese features over the next few forecast runs.\n\nDepending on where/when the aforementioned cold front moves over \nthe region, models are in some agreement with the front dropping \nsomewhere over the state and remaining nearly stationary, leading \nto additional chances for showers and storms on Tuesday. Another \nshortwave then looks to move over the region sometime midweek, \nbringing more chances for showers and storms.\n\nTemperatures...Highs will be a few degrees below normal today due to \nthe anticipated rain/cloud cover, with values in the low to mid 70s. \nTemperatures will then rise above normal on Sunday into the upper \n70s to mid 80s, and even warmer on Monday and Tuesday with highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s, due to the persistent southerly flow. This \nflow will also result in the return of humid weather on Sunday and \nMonday as dew points increase into the upper 60s and low 70s. \nDepending on cloud cover and precip chances, heat indices on Monday \nmay rise into the low to mid 90s. Mother Nature is reminding us \nsummer isn't over yet.",
